 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444047 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143454" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444047 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444047 #edb/ref 1071 "The scores in a standardized math test used for university admissions in a certain country are normally distributed with a mean of $57$ and a standard deviation of $16.$ Suppose $50$ test results are selected at random. What is the probability that the mean score for this sample will be less than $55?$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1469\\,$ | $\\,0.1446\\,$ | $\\,0.1423\\,$ | $\\,0.1401\\,$ | $\\,0.1379\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1711\\,$ | $\\,0.1685\\,$ | $\\,0.1660\\,$ | $\\,0.1635\\,$ | $\\,0.1611\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.1977\\,$ | $\\,0.1949\\,$ | $\\,0.1922\\,$ | $\\,0.1894\\,$ | $\\,0.1867\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444047 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "823af782-7946-51f9-98a3-ec3946da49d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444047 #edb/ref 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\n then the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nWe're told that the population mean $\\mu =57$ the population standard deviation $\\sigma =16,$ and that the sample size $n=50.$ Therefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(57,\\frac{{16}^{2}}{50}) \\\\  & ∼N(57,5.12).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<55).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<55) & =P(Z<\\frac{55-57}{\\sqrt{5.12}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-0.88) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.88)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.88)=0.1894.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<55) & ≈Φ(-0.88) \\\\  & =0.1894.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444048 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444048 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143293" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444048 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444048 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{64}∼N(-14,{16}^{2})$ is a random sample of size $64$ from a normal population, calculate $P(\\overset{―}{X}>-14.86).$\n\n*Hint: A value table of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.3015\\,$ | $\\,0.2981\\,$ | $\\,0.2946\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.3372\\,$ | $\\,0.3336\\,$ | $\\,0.3300\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3745\\,$ | $\\,0.3707\\,$ | $\\,0.3669\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444048 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a7acd22b-bdbb-5367-8608-dbbe55d9e96d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444048 #edb/ref 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(-14,\\frac{{16}^{2}}{64}) \\\\  & ∼N(-14,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>-14.86).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>-14.86) & =P(Z>\\frac{-14.86-(-14)}{\\sqrt{4}}) \\\\  & =P(Z>-0.43).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the complement of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>-0.43) & =1-P(Z\\le -0.43) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.43).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.43)=0.3336.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>-14.86) & =1-Φ(-0.43) \\\\  & =1-0.3336 \\\\  & =0.6664.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444049 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444049 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143283" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444049 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444049 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{40}∼N(12,{20}^{2})$ is a random sample of size $40$ from a normal population, calculate $P(\\overset{―}{X}<11).$\n\n*Hint: A value table of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3783\\,$ | $\\,0.3745\\,$ | $\\,0.3707\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4168\\,$ | $\\,0.4129\\,$ | $\\,0.4090\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.4562\\,$ | $\\,0.4522\\,$ | $\\,0.4483\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444049 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "befb2dbc-22e5-50b6-b20b-e66d941203a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444049 #edb/ref 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(12,\\frac{{20}^{2}}{40}) \\\\  & ∼N(12,10).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<11).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<11) & =P(Z<\\frac{11-12}{\\sqrt{10}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-0.32) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.32)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.32)=0.3745.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<11) & =Φ(-0.32) \\\\  & =0.3745.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444050 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186444053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444050 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2d091501-26c6-5546-8cbf-ebdf8a8dbdee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444050 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186444049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444051 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186444054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444051 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6c8c0cbe-f520-5209-9dc4-1c4370a0a1b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444051 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186444045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444052 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186444051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444052 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "80d34189-07b0-5bf3-baec-8db1e26b7c5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444052 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186444048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444053 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186444052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444053 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c24ec26c-5b2c-55c0-98a7-3053251894f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444053 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186444046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444054 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f80192e4-19d4-5cec-8fba-68f343667683" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444054 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186444047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497828 #edb/ref 1080 "0.5284" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497828 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497828 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0d2d79f1-f5df-56c1-a632-32e686a02c5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497842 #edb/ref 1080 "0.4648" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [